    how: "Instead of a junction between two types of silicon, a Schottky diode uses a junction between a metal and silicon. Electrons need less energy to cross this barrier, which gives the lower forward voltage. There is also no store of extra charge to sweep out when it turns off, so it has practically no reverse-recovery time. The trade-offs are higher leakage current in reverse, from microamps to milliamps and rising steeply with temperature, and lower maximum reverse voltages, typically 20–100 V.",
