    summary: "A diode that gives off light when current flows. An efficient indicator and light source, but it always needs its current limited.",
    purpose: "A light-emitting diode turns electric current directly into light. LEDs serve as power indicators, status lights, displays, backlights, room lighting and infrared transmitters for remote controls. They are efficient, last tens of thousands of hours and switch in nanoseconds, so they can be dimmed with PWM (rapid on-off switching) or used to send data. Standard 3 mm and 5 mm indicator LEDs run at about 2–20 mA; high-power LEDs need 350 mA or more and a heatsink; addressable LEDs such as the WS2812 contain their own controller chip.",
    how: "An LED is a diode made from compound semiconductors such as gallium arsenide or indium gallium nitride. When electrons cross the junction and drop into holes, they release their energy as photons of light. The material's band gap sets the colour and also the forward voltage: infrared about 1.2–1.5 V, red about 1.8–2.2 V, yellow and orange about 2.0–2.2 V, green 2.0–3.2 V depending on type, blue and white about 2.8–3.4 V. Above that voltage the current rises steeply for a tiny increase, so an LED cannot limit its own current.",
