    purpose: "A photodiode produces a current directly proportional to the light falling on it, which makes it the most accurate and fastest simple light sensor. It is used in light meters, pulse oximeters, smoke detectors, optical encoders, infrared receivers and fibre-optic links; a solar cell is essentially a very large photodiode. Typical parts: the BPW34 (large 7.5 mm² area, sensitive from visible to near-infrared light), the SFH203 (fast, in a 5 mm LED-style package) and the BPW21 (filtered to match the eye's sensitivity, for lux meters).",
    how: "A photodiode is a P-N junction with a window. Photons absorbed near the junction free electrons and holes, and the junction's built-in electric field sweeps them apart, producing a current called the photocurrent. It is tiny: a BPW34 gives about 50 µA at 1000 lux (bright indoor light or overcast daylight) and nanoamps in a dim room. The output is very linear over many decades of brightness. Reverse-biasing the diode (photoconductive mode) makes it faster by reducing its capacitance; at zero bias (photovoltaic mode) it has the least noise and leakage, called dark current.",
