    purpose: "The NPN bipolar junction transistor (BJT) is the workhorse of discrete electronics. As a switch, it lets a microcontroller pin turn on an LED string, relay, buzzer or small motor needing far more current than the pin can supply. As an amplifier, it boosts weak signals in audio and sensor circuits. Common parts: the 2N2222A (about 600 mA) and 2N3904 (200 mA) small-signal types, the BC547 (100 mA, with a European pin order), the TIP31C (3 A power transistor) and the TIP120 (5 A Darlington, with high gain but a larger voltage drop).",
    how: "An NPN transistor is a sandwich: a thin P-type base between an N-type collector and emitter. A small current flowing into the base and out of the emitter allows a much larger current to flow from collector to emitter, typically 100–300 times more for small transistors; this ratio is the gain, written β or hFE. Think of a large water valve opened by a small trickle. The base-emitter junction behaves like a diode, needing about 0.6–0.7 V to conduct. Fully on (saturated), the collector-emitter voltage falls to about 0.1–0.3 V, though Darlingtons such as the TIP120 drop 1–2 V.",
