    summary: "A MOSFET that turns on when its gate is pulled below its source. Ideal for switching positive supplies and for low-loss reverse-polarity protection.",
    purpose: "A P-channel MOSFET is the complement of the N-channel: its source connects to the positive supply, and it turns on when the gate is pulled several volts below the source. That makes it ideal for high-side switching, turning the positive supply on and off while the load stays grounded, and for neat power tricks: reverse-polarity protection with almost no voltage loss, and switching a load between USB and battery power (load sharing). Parts: the AO3401 (SOT-23, about 4 A, low gate voltage), the IRF9540N (100 V, 23 A) and the IRF4905 (55 V, 74 A); the last two need about 10 V of gate drive.",
    how: "It works like an N-MOSFET with every polarity reversed: a negative gate-to-source voltage forms a channel of holes between source and drain. Holes move more slowly than electrons, so a P-MOSFET has about two to three times the on-resistance of an N-MOSFET of the same size and cost, which is why designers prefer N-channel parts for heavy currents where possible. Like all MOSFETs it has a body diode, here pointing from drain to source, which conducts whenever the drain is higher than the source. Protection circuits make clever use of this.",
    integration: "High-side switch: source to the positive supply, drain to the load, and a 10–100 kΩ pull-up from gate to source to keep it off. Pull the gate low to turn it on; if the supply is higher than the logic voltage, use a small NPN or N-MOSFET to pull the gate down. Gates are usually rated ±20 V, so on supplies above about 15 V add a Zener and a resistor to limit gate drive. For reverse-polarity protection, put it in the positive line with drain to the battery, source to the circuit and gate to ground: connected correctly, the body diode conducts, then the channel turns on and bypasses it.",
