    summary: "The physical ways to connect parts: solderless breadboards for quick experiments, perfboard and PCBs for permanent builds, and wire sized for its current.",
    purpose: "Before a circuit works it has to be built. Solderless breadboards let you push parts and jumper wires into spring contacts and change things in seconds, which is perfect for prototyping. Solderable breadboards copy the same layout for a permanent version. Perfboard (holes with individual copper pads) and stripboard (pads joined in long strips) let you build sturdy one-offs. Printed circuit boards (PCBs) are best for anything compact, fast, or built more than once. Wire matters too: thin jumpers are fine for signals, but motors, LED strips and power need thicker wire.",
    how: "A standard breadboard has rows of five holes on each side of a centre channel; each row of five is joined by a spring clip underneath. The channel separates the sides so a chip can straddle it, giving each pin its own row. Long rails for power and ground run along the edges, sometimes broken halfway. Each contact adds a few to tens of milliohms of resistance, and neighbouring strips have a few picofarads of capacitance between them. Wire resistance rises with length and falls with thickness: 22 AWG copper is about 53 mΩ per metre, 18 AWG about 21 mΩ.",
    integration: "Use solid-core 22 AWG (0.64 mm) wire on breadboards; stranded wire frays and will not stay in. Keep each breadboard contact under about 1 A; beyond that, contacts heat up and voltages drop. For a permanent build, copy the layout onto a solderable breadboard, or plan a perfboard layout on paper first. As a conservative guide for short hookup wiring: 24 AWG about 1 A, 22 AWG about 1.5 A, 20 AWG about 3 A, 18 AWG about 5 A. Cheap Dupont jumpers are often 26–28 AWG, sometimes copper-clad aluminium, so keep them for signals.",
